WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.002
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.002 --> 00:00:08.382
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; It was the end of a very stressful

00:00:08.382 --> 00:00:11.070
summer, and I was not at my peak.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.510
I'd been working 60 hour weeks,
I wasn't feeling appreciated at work, and

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:17.980
I said that's it.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:19.240
I need a vacation.

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:22.125
Can anyone else here relate with that?

00:00:22.125 --> 00:00:23.730
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Everyone.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:27.300
The irony was is that I was
in the vacation business.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:30.700
We need vacations.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:36.540
They give us better health,
they make us more productive at work.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.680
And studies show that the best ones
are when we go further away from home,

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.300
and when we take more time off.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.990
So we should go travelling on vacation.

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:48.017
Problem is,
we don't get a lot of vacation days.

00:00:48.017 --> 00:00:51.083
Out of 137 countries
that mandate paid leave,

00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:53.350
the United States is not one of them.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.426
The European union requires
20 days of paid vacation.

00:00:57.426 --> 00:01:03.704
France and Finland get 30,
Afghanistan gets 35 days of paid leave.

00:01:03.704 --> 00:01:08.530
So we should be furious,
we're being deprived of our best days.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.080
There's a sad reality.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.072
We don't even take the vacation
days that are given to us.

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:20.760
In 2014, 41% of Americans
didn't take a single day off.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:25.700
In that year we left 500 million
days on the table, unused.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.565
I don't get this, I love vacation.

00:01:30.565 --> 00:01:33.600
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So much, that when I graduated college

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.615
I moved to a ski town and I pretty
much made vacation my full-time job.

00:01:38.615 --> 00:01:41.480
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Maybe people

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.190
aren't seeing the value
in vacation that I see.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.050
When I saw study that said that people are
happier when they're planning a vacation

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:53.600
than when they're actually on it,
I knew there was a problem.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:58.800
To illustrate this,
let's go on a vacation together.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.110
So we start planning.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:05.370
We look for inspiration, we look at
private beaches, always sunny days.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:08.156
You see a gorgeous couple
falling madly in love.

00:02:08.156 --> 00:02:09.550
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In the sunset.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.250
And you think, that could be me.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:16.480
I just have to go from point a,
boring stressful home or school, and

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.985
go to point b, where the land
is magical and everyone's happy.

00:02:19.985 --> 00:02:22.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So we start our trip.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.430
Flight was only slightly delayed,
most of your bags made it.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:30.305
And then you get in the taxi and
you're pretty sure you got ripped off.

00:02:30.305 --> 00:02:33.135
Siete means $78, right?

00:02:33.135 --> 00:02:37.959
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But the hotel pool is just as promised.

00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:41.079
You get to your room, though,
and your toilet looks like this.

00:02:43.726 --> 00:02:45.060
So you're stressed out.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.050
You sit down in your chair,
you pull out your phone,

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:53.560
and if you're like 70% of people on
vacation, you check your work email.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:54.980
And that really angers you.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:57.847
So then you go to Instagram and
you realize, my gosh,

00:02:57.847 --> 00:03:00.663
my friends are having a much
better vacation than I am.

00:03:00.663 --> 00:03:04.517
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:09.324
are now experiencing what we in the travel
industry like to call disappointment.

00:03:09.324 --> 00:03:11.082
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:03:11.082 --> 00:03:14.860
this is when your expectations
are much higher than your reality.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.370
I asked my business school friends,
why do you all like to go on vacation?

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:24.620
And the overwhelming response
was that they like to relax.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:29.870
Why would anyone want to go on vacation,
if that was your reality?

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.680
My private equity friend said
that she loves to go on cruises

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.940
because she gets to chill out and
do nothing.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.500
My consultant friend echoed this,
and he said that his days are so

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:45.370
hectic that he just likes to
disconnect and do nothing.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.480
There has to be something more
to vacation than nothing.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:54.990
What I was hearing from my classmates
is that they were treating vacation

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:58.760
as a painkiller to deal with
the psychological stress of their work,

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.430
when I could see vacation as
a vitamin to enrich their life.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.082
But how could I convey this
to my consultant friend,

00:04:06.082 --> 00:04:07.480
he spoke a foreign language.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:08.585
So, I made a two by two.

00:04:08.585 --> 00:04:13.140
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:18.510
we decided that the best trips were
both pleasurable and memorable.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:22.130
And we would plot the data based on
how you would answer the question,

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:23.056
how was your vacation?

00:04:23.056 --> 00:04:26.680
So the bottom left quadrant, we've
got the one that we planned together.

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:29.825
It was okay.

00:04:29.825 --> 00:04:33.644
And then on the top left, private equity
girl's dream, all-inclusive trip.

00:04:33.644 --> 00:04:35.125
You say, aaaah, but

00:04:35.125 --> 00:04:40.400
it's not a trip you're going to
tell your kids about in ten years.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.070
Now, the bottom left quadrant,
this is the danger zone.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:46.750
This is where, hypothetically,
you have to spend the night with

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:49.580
the Russian Federal Police and
then the next day, on the way home,

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:53.200
you get strip searched,
not once, but twice, in Israel.

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:57.550
Don't ask.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:58.863
&gt;&gt; The top left,

00:04:58.863 --> 00:05:03.357
that must be what we're looking for,
amazing.

00:05:03.357 --> 00:05:06.848
As I reflected back on my
own travel experiences,

00:05:06.848 --> 00:05:10.682
I realized that the majority
of my trips were amazing.

00:05:10.682 --> 00:05:14.107
But some were better than others,
some were more impactful, and

00:05:14.107 --> 00:05:16.360
I couldn't put my finger on what that was.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:23.030
No textbook on the tourism industry
would be complete without a chapter on

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:27.200
Abraham Maslow, a psychologist
who studied motivation in humans.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.470
He created the hierarchy of needs
to say that people inherently seek

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:35.300
self- actualization, when they feel like
they're reaching their full potential.

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:38.690
And along the way,
they must meet certain needs.

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:42.870
And when they do, they're more likely
to have what he calls peak experiences.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:46.930
He defines these as moments
of the highest happiness,

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:50.400
where people feel like they've
found their place in the world.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.770
I sent these definitions off to
the student body at the GSB, and

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:56.720
I asked them,
have you ever had any peak experiences?

00:05:58.040 --> 00:05:59.350
The answers were great.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:00.280
This is just a snippet.

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:05.560
One of them was from someone who
served in Afghanistan as a pilot.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:08.790
And after a particularly challenging
mission he said he literally felt

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:10.330
a calming state of nirvana.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:17.970
This was impactful because, of all the
responses I got, his was the only one that

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:23.080
described a time when he was at work, and
he was serving our country in Afghanistan.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:29.080
These are the moments that
make life worth living for.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:33.340
When you feel so much joy and
love and you feel like you've

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:37.150
found your place in this world,
this is exactly what I was looking for

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:40.610
that defined the amazing trips and
the transformational trips.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:46.380
If we can't find these peak
experiences when we're at work,

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:50.290
when will we, when will you?

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:56.640
Getting the most out of your
vacation takes a lot of practice and

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.210
a little bit of knowledge.

00:06:58.210 --> 00:06:59.850
And to help you with that,

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:03.365
I created a framework,
Gabby's hierarchy of vacation needs.

00:07:03.365 --> 00:07:07.425
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And at the base,

00:07:07.425 --> 00:07:11.979
at the minimum, you need to disconnect and
take your vacation days.

00:07:11.979 --> 00:07:16.764
Next, you need to understand that too
many expectations are setting you up for

00:07:16.764 --> 00:07:19.239
disaster because things will go wrong.

00:07:19.239 --> 00:07:22.870
And when they do, you want to try and
find a little bit of pleasure in them.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.380
But when the things go right,
you want to make sure that

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:30.590
those are the memorable experiences,
and you remember them.

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:35.880
And when you do, you'll be set up for
peak experiences.

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:41.950
My trips have taken me all over the world,
and so much has gone wrong.

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:46.198
I've lost dozens of passports, literally.

00:07:46.198 --> 00:07:50.189
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And yes I've been strip search in

00:07:50.189 --> 00:07:54.891
Israel, but I've also had
those rare moments of joy and

00:07:54.891 --> 00:07:59.810
happiness where I have felt in wonder and
awe of the world.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:06.241
My hope for you is that you have on-time

00:08:06.241 --> 00:08:09.855
departures, and
toilets that you're familiar with.

00:08:09.855 --> 00:08:13.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And that you have many,

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:18.290
many peak experiences along the way.

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:21.039
I hope you enjoy your vacation.

00:08:21.039 --> 00:08:21.990
Thank you.

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:24.196
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:24.196 --> 00:08:29.149
[MUSIC]

